:# AGENTS.md

# Portfolio — Architecture × Software × Computational Design

This repository contains a personal portfolio at the intersection of:

- Architecture
- Software development
- Computational design
- Geometry
- Technical drawing
- Digital experimentation

This is not a generic developer portfolio and must never evolve into one.

The website should feel like an architectural drawing set, experimental
editorial publication, technical interface and computational design system
combined into a single coherent visual language.

The project is intentionally visual, experimental and highly designed.

Code quality matters.

Visual quality matters equally.

---

# 1. CORE PRINCIPLE

Do not optimize for the shortest valid solution.

For this project, "working" is not the same as "finished".

A technically correct implementation that looks generic, unfinished,
overly simple or visually weak is considered incomplete.

When implementing frontend work, prefer:

- complete solutions
- polished compositions
- intentional visual hierarchy
- robust responsive behavior
- meaningful interaction
- refined typography
- visual consistency
- carefully considered details

over:

- minimal patches
- shortest possible code
- generic components
- quick approximations
- visually neutral solutions

It is acceptable and expected to write substantial HTML, CSS and JavaScript
when the design benefits from it.

Do not reduce implementation depth merely to keep the diff small.

---

# 2. IMPLEMENTATION DEPTH & QUALITY BAR

This project has a high frontend quality bar.

Treat important visual tasks as full design implementations, not small UI patches.

For every major visual section or redesign:

1. Inspect the existing implementation.
2. Understand the visual language already present.
3. Identify the intended composition.
4. Implement the complete solution.
5. Add appropriate interaction and motion.
6. Check responsive behavior.
7. Check accessibility.
8. Perform a visual refinement pass.
9. Fix anything that feels generic, accidental or unfinished.
10. Only then consider the task complete.

Do not stop at the first technically working version.

Perform at least one refinement pass after the initial implementation.

During refinement, actively ask:

- Does this look generic?
- Does this look like a template?
- Is the composition intentional?
- Is the hierarchy strong enough?
- Is spacing deliberate?
- Does typography contribute to the design?
- Does mobile feel designed or merely compressed?
- Are interactions meaningful?
- Are there unnecessary decorations?
- Could architectural or computational concepts strengthen the composition?
- Does this section belong to the same visual system as the rest of the site?

If the answer reveals a weakness, improve it before finishing.

---

# 3. DO NOT BE MINIMALIST BY DEFAULT

Do not interpret concise user requests as requests for minimal implementations.

For example:

"Improve the hero"

does NOT mean:

- change two font sizes
- add one animation
- modify a background
- return immediately

It means:

- inspect the hero
- understand its role
- evaluate composition
- evaluate typography
- evaluate technical graphics
- evaluate interactions
- evaluate responsive behavior
- improve the complete experience where appropriate

Similarly:

"Improve the project cards"

does not necessarily mean preserving the existing card structure.

If the current visual structure is weak, redesign it.

Minimal diffs are NOT a priority for visual redesign tasks.

Do not preserve weak code or weak compositions merely to minimize changes.

---

# 4. TECHNOLOGY CONSTRAINTS

The site must remain lightweight and directly deployable to GitHub Pages.

Use only:

- HTML5
- CSS3
- Vanilla JavaScript
- SVG
- native browser APIs

Unless the user explicitly requests otherwise, DO NOT introduce:

- React
- Vue
- Angular
- Svelte
- Tailwind
- Bootstrap
- jQuery
- GSAP
- Three.js
- npm packages
- build tools
- bundlers
- package managers
- external UI frameworks
- unnecessary dependencies

The default architecture must continue to work by opening or serving
`index.html`.

The project should remain suitable for static deployment.

---

# 5. PROJECT STRUCTURE

Prefer a simple structure similar to:

/
├── AGENTS.md
├── README.md
├── index.html
├── styles.css
├── script.js
├── docs/
│   ├── DESIGN_SYSTEM.md
│   ├── INTERACTIONS.md
│   └── CONTENT_GUIDE.md
└── assets/
    ├── architecture/
    ├── software/
    ├── references/
    ├── icons/
    └── portfolio.pdf

Do not introduce unnecessary architectural complexity.

If the project grows significantly, additional files may be introduced
when they improve maintainability.

Do not split files merely for the sake of abstraction.

---

# 6. VISUAL IDENTITY

The visual identity is based on the intersection of:

ARCHITECTURAL DRAWING
×
DESCRIPTIVE GEOMETRY
×
COMPUTATIONAL DESIGN
×
EDITORIAL DESIGN
×
SOFTWARE INTERFACES

The website should feel precise, technical and experimental.

It should not feel like a conventional commercial website.

Primary visual references include:

- architectural presentation sheets
- construction drawings
- sections
- elevations
- plans
- axonometric drawings
- descriptive geometry
- registration marks
- dimension lines
- construction lines
- coordinates
- drawing indexes
- sheet numbering
- legends
- annotations
- technical manuals
- modular systems
- computational diagrams
- CAD-like interfaces
- experimental editorial layouts

Use these concepts intelligently.

Do not turn them into superficial decoration.

---

# 7. VISUAL CHARACTER

The desired character is:

- technical
- architectural
- precise
- experimental
- futuristic
- editorial
- computational
- elegant
- controlled
- slightly raw
- visually distinctive

The website may be unconventional.

It must not become confusing.

Experimentation should exist inside a strong information hierarchy.

---

# 8. COLOR SYSTEM

Primary palette:

- near black
- off-white
- white
- neutral gray
- signal red
- technical blue

Red is the primary accent.

Blue is secondary.

Avoid using both accents everywhere.

Color should behave like architectural annotation:

- purposeful
- sparse
- informative
- directional

Prefer a controlled accent over decorative color noise.

Good uses for red:

- registration marks
- selected states
- drawing references
- numbers
- important links
- active navigation
- technical annotations
- interaction feedback

Good uses for blue:

- coordinate systems
- secondary technical information
- diagrams
- computational information
- selected geometry
- occasional large conceptual sections

Do not introduce additional saturated colors without a strong reason.

---

# 9. BACKGROUNDS

The site may alternate between:

- near-black sections
- off-white drawing-sheet sections
- monochrome image fields
- occasional technical-blue compositions

Transitions between light and dark sections should feel intentional.

Think of them as different drawing sheets, chapters or modes.

Do not alternate backgrounds arbitrarily.

---

# 10. TYPOGRAPHY

Typography should contribute strongly to the visual identity.

Prefer combinations of:

- narrow sans-serif
- neutral grotesk
- monospace
- editorial serif

Use typography hierarchically.

Possible roles:

SANS:
- navigation
- large architectural titles
- project names
- structural information

MONO:
- coordinates
- metadata
- years
- dimensions
- labels
- project numbers
- technical annotations

SERIF:
- conceptual statements
- editorial moments
- references
- selected large text

Typography may use extreme scale differences.

For example:

- very large project/title typography
- tiny technical annotations

This contrast is encouraged when controlled.

Avoid making every text element medium-sized.

Do not overuse uppercase indiscriminately.

Uppercase works particularly well for technical labels and architectural
drawing metadata.

---

# 11. LAYOUT PHILOSOPHY

Do not default to centered containers with repeated identical cards.

Prefer architectural composition.

Use:

- asymmetric grids
- deliberate empty space
- offset elements
- large image fields
- technical margins
- vertical labels
- drawing numbers
- overlapping lines
- controlled alignment breaks
- sectional compositions
- editorial rhythm
- modular systems
- extreme scale contrast

Whitespace is part of the design.

Do not fill every available area.

A section should sometimes feel like a drawing sheet rather than a webpage.

---

# 12. GRID SYSTEM

A visible or implied technical grid may be used throughout the site.

The grid should help:

- align content
- structure sections
- establish rhythm
- connect visual elements
- create architectural precision

Grid lines should generally be thin and subtle.

Avoid turning the entire site into visually noisy graph paper.

The user should feel the grid before necessarily noticing it.

---

# 13. TECHNICAL GRAPHICS

Technical graphics are encouraged.

Examples:

- crosshairs
- reticles
- axes
- coordinate systems
- measurement lines
- arrows
- north symbols
- drawing scales
- diagram nodes
- modular grids
- construction circles
- Bézier-like paths
- section markers
- elevation markers
- index numbers
- dashed construction lines
- projection lines
- technical legends

Prefer CSS and SVG for these graphics.

Use SVG when the geometry benefits from precise vector control.

Do not add technical graphics randomly.

Every technical element should contribute to:

- composition
- hierarchy
- interaction
- identity
- information

---

# 14. AVOID GENERIC WEB DESIGN

Never introduce generic design patterns without evaluating them critically.

Avoid:

- SaaS-style cards
- generic dashboards
- excessive rounded rectangles
- giant pill buttons
- generic gradient backgrounds
- purple/blue startup gradients
- glassmorphism
- random blur blobs
- excessive shadows
- neon cyberpunk aesthetics
- generic "developer portfolio" styling
- terminal windows used as decoration
- fake code snippets used only for aesthetics
- excessive icon grids
- generic feature-card layouts
- identical three-column card sections
- unnecessary carousels
- stock illustrations
- template-looking hero sections

Border radius should be used sparingly.

Most architectural elements should prefer:

- sharp edges
- fine borders
- precise geometry

---

# 15. PROJECT PRESENTATION

Architecture projects should NOT simply appear as identical cards.

Think of each project as a small architectural sheet.

A project presentation may contain:

- project number
- title
- year
- location
- typology
- scale
- role
- drawing type
- image
- plan
- section
- diagram
- short description
- technical metadata

Different projects may use different compositions.

Variation is encouraged when the overall system remains coherent.

For example:

PROJECT 01 could emphasize an axonometric image.

PROJECT 02 could behave like a descriptive geometry sheet.

PROJECT 03 could use a large section drawing.

PROJECT 04 could use a modular instruction/manual layout.

Avoid repeating the exact same layout for every project.

---

# 16. ARCHITECTURE SECTION

The architecture section should feel closest to an actual architecture portfolio.

Prioritize:

- drawings
- images
- diagrams
- technical information
- composition

over long descriptive paragraphs.

When imagery exists, give it enough visual importance.

Use metadata to reinforce the technical-document feeling.

Architecture projects may transition between light and dark drawing-sheet modes.

Do not turn architecture projects into blog posts.

---

# 17. SOFTWARE SECTION

Programming must belong to the same universe as architecture.

Do not suddenly switch to a generic GitHub/developer aesthetic.

Software projects should be presented as:

- systems
- tools
- experiments
- prototypes
- computational processes
- spatial algorithms
- geometry studies
- interfaces
- generators

Useful visual elements include:

- nodes
- grids
- generated geometry
- SVG diagrams
- parameters
- inputs/outputs
- system states
- coordinate fields
- algorithmic structures

Code can appear when meaningful.

Do not use fake terminal windows purely for decoration.

---

# 18. ARCHITECTURE × SOFTWARE

Architecture and programming should feel like two expressions of the same practice.

Whenever appropriate, emphasize concepts shared by both disciplines:

- systems
- constraints
- geometry
- structure
- hierarchy
- modules
- coordinates
- transformations
- iteration
- rules
- networks
- spatial logic
- parameters
- generative processes

This conceptual bridge is central to the portfolio.

---

# 19. HERO SECTION

The hero is a major visual piece.

It must not look like a generic:

NAME
JOB TITLE
BUTTON
PHOTO

landing-page hero.

Prefer the feeling of:

- an architectural cover sheet
- a drawing index
- a computational interface
- an experimental publication cover

The name/brand should have strong visual presence.

Supporting elements may include:

- coordinates
- edition/version
- location
- discipline
- drawing number
- technical geometry
- construction lines
- SVG diagrams
- index labels
- controlled animation

Do not overcrowd the hero.

The primary hierarchy must remain obvious.

---

# 20. MOTION PHILOSOPHY

Motion is part of the visual system.

Animation should feel:

- precise
- mechanical
- geometric
- controlled
- responsive

Avoid:

- bouncy animations
- playful app-like easing
- excessive spring physics
- animation on every element
- meaningless continuous motion

Prefer:

- line drawing
- clipping
- masking
- coordinate movement
- subtle parallax
- controlled scrambling
- progressive reveals
- image shifts
- technical scanning
- geometry transformations
- directional movement

Animation should reinforce architecture/computation.

---

# 21. MICROINTERACTIONS

Interactive elements should respond clearly but subtly.

Potential interactions:

- technical cursor
- crosshair cursor
- coordinates following pointer position
- magnetic links
- image displacement
- line extensions
- annotation reveals
- changing drawing numbers
- grid responses
- small SVG transformations
- controlled text scrambling

Do not use every interaction simultaneously.

Hierarchy applies to motion too.

Major interactions should remain memorable because minor elements are quieter.

---

# 22. CUSTOM CURSOR

A custom cursor is allowed on devices with accurate pointing devices.

It should:

- remain lightweight
- preserve usability
- provide clear interaction feedback
- disappear or adapt appropriately over controls
- never prevent clicking
- never introduce pointer lag

Do not force a custom cursor on touch devices.

Use pointer/hover media queries where appropriate.

---

# 23. SCROLL INTERACTIONS

Scroll-based animation should use performant browser techniques.

Prefer:

- IntersectionObserver
- CSS transforms
- opacity
- clipping
- CSS custom properties

Avoid continuously querying layout unnecessarily.

Use `requestAnimationFrame` for pointer/scroll animation when appropriate.

Do not attach expensive unthrottled scroll handlers.

---

# 24. JAVASCRIPT PHILOSOPHY

JavaScript should progressively enhance the design.

The page should remain understandable if JavaScript fails.

Prefer:

- small focused functions
- clear state
- event delegation where useful
- IntersectionObserver
- requestAnimationFrame
- modern DOM APIs

Avoid:

- unnecessary global variables
- repeated DOM queries inside animation loops
- layout thrashing
- enormous monolithic event handlers
- unnecessary abstraction

Do not write JavaScript simply because something can be written in JavaScript.

Use CSS when CSS is sufficient.

---

# 25. CSS PHILOSOPHY

CSS is a major part of this project.

Do not be afraid of substantial CSS when required for visual quality.

Prefer:

- CSS custom properties
- Grid
- Flexbox
- clamp()
- min()
- max()
- aspect-ratio
- logical organization
- pseudo-elements
- modern selectors when browser support is reasonable
- media queries
- pointer capability queries
- prefers-reduced-motion

Avoid:

- unnecessary `!important`
- magic numbers without reason
- duplicated declarations
- excessively specific selectors
- inline styles unless values are intentionally data-driven

Use reusable primitives where they improve consistency.

Do not abstract unique editorial compositions into generic components
if doing so weakens the design.

---

# 26. CSS CUSTOM PROPERTIES

Centralize important design tokens.

Examples:

```css
:root {
  --color-black: #080808;
  --color-white: #f2f0ea;
  --color-gray: #8b8b86;
  --color-red: #ff3b30;
  --color-blue: #4a8dff;

  --line: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);

  --page-padding: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 4rem);
  --section-space: clamp(7rem, 14vw, 14rem);

  --font-sans: "Arial Narrow", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
  --font-mono: "Courier New", Courier, monospace;
  --font-serif: "Times New Roman", Times, serif;
}
